    INSERT INTO stage_mcqs (question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    ('62: "Endometriosis" is defined as:', '["Endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterine cavity", "Cancer of the endometrium", "Infection of the tubes", "Thickened uterine wall"]', 0, 'Most common sites include ovaries, uterosacral ligaments, and the pouch of Douglas. (Beckmann; Williams)'),
    ('63: What is the MOST common theory for the pathogenesis of endometriosis?', '["Coelomic metaplasia", "Halban''s Lymphatic spread", "Sampson''s theory (Retrograde Menstruation)", "Genetic mutation"]', 2, 'Suggests that menstrual blood containing viable endometrial cells flows backward through the tubes into the peritoneal cavity. (Beckmann; Williams)'),
    ('64: Which theory explains endometriosis in premenarchal girls or in the lungs?', '["Retrograde menstruation", "Coelomic Metaplasia / Lymphatic spread", "Infection", "None"]', 1, 'Coelomic metaplasia suggests peritoneal cells transform into endometrial cells; Halban''s theory suggests spread via lymphatics/veins. (Comprehensive Gynecology; Williams)'),
    ('65: Which of the following is a classic symptom of Endometriosis?', '["Secondary dysmenorrhea", "Deep dyspareunia", "Dyschezia (pain with defecation)", "All of the above"]', 3, 'The "3 Ds" of endometriosis are hallmark symptoms. (Blueprints; Beckmann)'),
    ('66: A 26-year-old woman presents with chronic pelvic pain that is worse BEFORE menses and improves after. On exam, her uterus is fixed and retroverted. What is the likely diagnosis?', '["Fibroids", "Endometriosis", "PCOS", "Pelvic Inflammatory Disease"]', 1, 'Chronic inflammation leads to adhesions, causing a fixed, retroverted uterus. (Beckmann; ACOG)'),
    ('67: What is a characteristic pelvic exam finding in advanced endometriosis?', '["Symmetrically enlarged uterus", "Nodularity and tenderness of the uterosacral ligaments", "Cervical motion tenderness", "Soft cervix"]', 1, 'Endometrial implants on the uterosacral ligaments are often palpable and very tender. (Williams; Blueprints)'),
    ('68: An "Endometrioma" (Chocolate Cyst) of the ovary typically shows what appearance on ultrasound?', '["Purely anechoic/black", "Ground-glass appearance (diffuse low-level internal echoes)", "Solid and calcified", "Fluid-filled with septations"]', 1, 'The ground-glass appearance is due to old, thick blood within the cyst. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('69: What is the "Gold Standard" for the diagnosis of endometriosis?', '["MRI", "CA-125 levels", "Laparoscopy with direct visualization (and ideally biopsy)", "Pelvic Ultrasound"]', 2, 'Visual inspection of the pelvis via laparoscopy is the only way to definitively diagnose and stage the disease. (Beckmann; ACOG)'),
    ('70: Visually, early "active" endometriotic implants often appear as:', '["White scars", "Red, petechial, or flame-like lesions", "Black \"powder-burn\" lesions", "Clear vesicles"]', 1, 'Red lesions are more metabolically active and produce more prostaglandins. (Comprehensive Gynecology; Williams)'),
    ('71: Late, "inactive" or scarred endometriotic implants classically appear as:', '["Bright red", "Blue-black \"powder-burn\" or \"mulberry\" spots", "Yellow spots", "None"]', 1, 'Classic board description for visual diagnosis. (Beckmann; Blueprints)'),
    ('72: What is the role of CA-125 in the management of endometriosis?', '["It is a diagnostic tool", "It may be elevated but is non-specific; it is NOT recommended for routine screening or diagnosis", "It is always zero", "None"]', 1, 'While often elevated in endometriosis, its lack of specificity makes it poor for diagnosis. (Williams; ACOG)'),
    ('73: In the ASRM staging system for endometriosis, which factor contributes MOST to "Stage IV" disease?', '["Small superficial implants", "Dense adhesions and large endometriomas", "Pain intensity", "Patient age"]', 1, 'ASRM staging is based on the anatomy/extent of disease, not the severity of symptoms. (Beckmann; Williams)'),
    ('74: Is the severity of pelvic pain in endometriosis correlated with the Stage (I-IV) of the disease?', '["Yes, Stage IV is the most painful", "No, there is often a POOR correlation between symptom severity and disease extent", "Only in teens", "None"]', 1, 'A woman with Stage I can have debilitating pain, while a woman with Stage IV may be asymptomatic (often found during infertility workup). (Blueprints; Williams)'),
    ('75: What is the FIRST-LINE medical treatment for suspected endometriosis with mild-to-moderate symptoms?', '["GnRH agonists", "NSAIDs and Combined Oral Contraceptives (COCs)", "Laparoscopy", "Hysterectomy"]', 1, 'Symptomatic management is preferred before invasive procedures. (ACOG; Beckmann)'),
    ('76: How do Combined Oral Contraceptives (COCs) help in endometriosis?', '["They cure the disease", "They induce a \"pseudopregnancy\" state, causing atrophy of the implants", "They block FSH only", "None"]', 1, 'Progestins cause decidualization and atrophy of the ectopic tissue. (Blueprints; Williams)'),
    ('77: "Progestin-only" therapies (e.g., Medroxyprogesterone, Norethindrone) treat endometriosis by:', '["Blocking estrogen and causing decidualization/atrophy of implants", "Stimulating ovulation", "Increasing LH", "None"]', 0, 'Highly effective for pain relief. (Beckmann; ACOG)'),
    ('78: "Pseudomenopause" state to treat endometriosis?', '["OCPs", "GnRH agonists (e.g., Leuprolide)", "NSAIDs", "Clomiphene"]', 1, 'By suppressing the HPO axis and inducing a hypoestrogenic state. (Beckmann; Williams)'),
    ('79: "Danazol" is an older treatment for endometriosis. What is its mechanism?', '["Pure estrogen", "Weak androgen that inhibits mid-cycle LH/FSH surge and creates a high-androgen/low-estrogen environment", "Antibiotic", "None"]', 1, 'Rarely used now due to significant virilizing side effects (acne, hirsutism, voice deepening). (Comprehensive Gynecology; Blueprints)'),
    ('80: "Elagolix" is a newer medication for endometriosis. What is its class?', '["GnRH agonist", "Oral GnRH antagonist", "Progestin", "Aromatase inhibitor"]', 1, 'Provides dose-dependent suppression of estrogen without the initial flare seen with agonists. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('81: "Aromatase Inhibitors" (e.g., Letrozole) are sometimes used off-label for endometriosis because:', '["They increase estrogen", "Endometriotic implants express aromatase and produce their own estrogen; inhibitors block this local production", "They prevent pregnancy", "None"]', 1, 'Used in refractory cases. (Williams; ACOG)'),
    ('82: When is SURGERY indicated for endometriosis?', '["Immediately upon diagnosis", "Failure of medical therapy, severe symptoms, or large endometriomas (> 4-5 cm)", "To prevent cancer in all patients", "None"]', 1, 'Laparoscopic excision or ablation is the surgical treatment of choice. (Beckmann; ACOG)'),
    ('83: What is the effect of "surgical excision/ablation" of endometriosis on fertility?', '["No effect", "It significantly improves pregnancy rates in women with Stage I/II disease", "It causes permanent sterility", "None"]', 1, 'Restoring anatomy and reducing inflammation helps fertility. (Blueprints; Williams)'),
    ('84: A woman has an 8 cm endometrioma. What is the preferred surgical management?', '["Simple drainage (aspiration)", "Cystectomy (excision of the cyst wall)", "Observation", "Oophorectomy in all cases"]', 1, 'Cystectomy is superior to drainage, as drainage has a nearly 100% recurrence rate. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('85: Definitive surgical treatment for endometriosis is:', '["Laparoscopic ablation", "Total Hysterectomy with Bilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (BSO)", "Unilateral Oophorectomy", "Myomectomy"]', 1, 'Removing the source of estrogen (ovaries) is the definitive step. (Beckmann; Blueprints)'),
    ('86: A 17-year-old with cyclic pelvic pain has failed OCPs and NSAIDs. What is the next step?', '["Hysterectomy", "Diagnostic laparoscopy to evaluate for endometriosis", "Wait until she is 21", "Psychiatric referral"]', 1, 'Endometriosis is the leading cause of chronic pelvic pain in adolescents failing medical therapy. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('87: Which of the following increases the risk of endometriosis?', '["Early menarche, short cycles (< 27 days), and long duration of flow", "Late menarche", "Multiparity", "Exercise"]', 0, 'Factors that increase total menstrual exposure. (Williams; Beckmann)'),
    ('88: Which of the following REDUCES the risk of endometriosis?', '["Multiparity and extended lactation", "Nulliparity", "Early menarche", "Smoking"]', 0, 'Lactation and presidency induce periods of amenorrhea/low estrogen. (Blueprints; Williams)'),
    ('89: "Catamenial Pneumothorax" is a rare manifestation of:', '["Asthma", "Thoracic Endometriosis", "Fibroids", "TB"]', 1, 'Recurrent pneumothorax occurring with menses due to diaphragmatic or pleural implants. (Williams; Comprehensive Gynecology)'),
    ('90: Why is "endometriosis" associated with an increased risk of specific ovarian cancers?', '["It is not associated", "Chronic inflammation and iron-induced oxidative stress may lead to Clear Cell and Endometrioid ovarian cancers", "It causes HPV", "None"]', 1, 'Known as "Endometriosis-Associated Ovarian Cancer" (EAOC). (Williams; ACOG)'),
    ('91: A woman with endometriosis has "cyclical hematuria." This suggests implants on the:', '["Ovaries", "Bladder", "Rectum", "Umbilicus"]', 1, 'Extrapelvic spread to the urinary tract. (Beckmann; Williams)'),
    ('92: A woman with endometriosis has "cyclical hematochezia" (rectal bleeding). This suggests implants on the:', '["Bladder", "Sigmoid colon or Rectum", "Small bowel", "None"]', 1, 'Bowel involvement occurs in ~5-15% of patients. (Beckmann; Blueprints)'),
    ('93: "Deep Infiltrating Endometriosis" (DIE) is defined as implants penetrating > _____ mm into the retroperitoneal space or organs.', '["1", "5", "10", "20"]', 1, 'Requires complex multidisciplinary surgery. (Williams; Comprehensive Gynecology)'),
    ('94: Which of the following is the MOST effective medical treatment for preventing recurrence of symptoms AFTER surgery for endometriosis?', '["Immediate pregnancy", "Postoperative hormonal suppression (e.g., LNG-IUD or OCPs)", "NSAIDs only", "None"]', 1, 'Surgery is not curative; hormonal suppression is vital to maintain results. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('95: In a patient with an endometrioma, why is "aspiration" (needle drainage) under ultrasound NOT recommended?', '["Risk of cancer spread", "High risk of infection and nearly 100% recurrence rate", "It is too expensive", "None"]', 1, 'The cyst wall remains and will quickly refill. (Beckmann; ACOG)'),
    ('96: Is there a genetic component to endometriosis?', '["No", "Yes, there is a 7-10 fold increased risk in first-degree relatives", "Only in twins", "None"]', 1, 'Polygenic and multifactorial inheritance. (Williams; Beckmann)'),
    ('97: Which biochemical feature is characteristic of endometriotic tissue compared to normal endometrium?', '["Decreased aromatase", "Overexpression of Aromatase and Progesterone resistance", "Low COX-2", "None"]', 1, 'Leads to local estrogen production and inflammation. (Williams; Comprehensive Gynecology)'),
    ('98: In a patient with suspected endometriosis, "dyspareunia" is most often "deep" and occurs due to:', '["Vaginal dryness", "Traction on tender uterosacral ligaments or a fixed retroverted uterus", "Anxiety", "None"]', 1, 'Mechanical pressure on implants during intercourse. (Blueprints; Beckmann)'),
    ('99: The "Allen-Masters Window" refers to:', '["A type of laparoscope", "A defect in the broad ligament peritoneum sometimes seen in endometriosis", "A surgical incision", "None"]', 1, 'Classic anatomical finding in some patients. (Williams)'),
    ('100: "Decidualization" of an endometrioma in a PREGNANT woman can make it look like _____ on ultrasound.', '["A dermoid", "Malignancy (due to vascular mural nodules)", "A simple cyst", "None"]', 1, 'Can be confusing and may require monitoring or MRI. (Williams)'),
    ('101: Which of the following is a common metabolic finding in women with endometriosis?', '["Low CA-125", "High levels of proinflammatory cytokines in peritoneal fluid", "High Progesterone", "None"]', 1, 'Increased TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6 contribute to pain and infertility. (Comprehensive Gynecology; Williams)'),
    ('102: "Endometriosis of the umbilicus" is also known as:', '["Sister Mary Joseph nodule", "Villar''s nodule", "Striae", "None"]', 1, 'Sister Mary Joseph is metastatic cancer; Villar''s is primary umbilical endometriosis. (Williams)'),
    ('103: A 30-year-old with infertility and endometriosis has patent tubes. What is the likely mechanism of infertility?', '["Blocked tubes", "Inflammatory peritoneal environment affecting sperm motility and egg-sperm interaction", "Lack of eggs", "None"]', 1, 'Even Stage I/II endometriosis can reduce fecundity. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('104: For a woman with endometriosis desiring pregnancy, is "GnRH agonist" therapy helpful for improving fertility?', '["Yes, it cleans the pelvis", "No, medical suppression does NOT improve pregnancy rates and only delays conception", "Only if taken for 5 years", "None"]', 1, 'Medical therapy prevents ovulation; surgery or IVF is needed for fertility. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('105: Which surgery is better for PAIN relief in endometriosis: ablation (burning) or excision (cutting out)?', '["Ablation", "Excision (especially for deep disease)", "They are equal", "None"]', 1, 'Excision ensures the entire depth of the implant is removed. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('106: "Uterosacral ligament suspension" is often avoided in deep endometriosis because:', '["It causes cancer", "The ligaments are often scarred and distorted by disease", "It is too easy", "None"]', 1, 'Requires careful dissection of the ureters. (Williams)'),
    ('107: "Endometriosis-related" chronic pelvic pain is defined as pain lasting at least:', '["1 month", "3 months", "6 months", "1 year"]', 2, 'Standard definition for CPP. (Beckmann)'),
    ('108: A woman with endometriosis on OCPs still has pain. What is the next logical medical step?', '["Hysterectomy", "Switch to a GnRH agonist or antagonist", "Double the OCP dose", "Aspirin"]', 1, 'Escalation of hormonal suppression. (ACOG; Blueprints)'),
    ('109: Which of the following is a potential complication of large endometriomas?', '["Spontaneous rupture causing acute chemical peritonitis", "Torsion", "Infection", "All of the above"]', 3, 'Though rupture is the most characteristic acute presentation. (Williams)'),
    ('110: "Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome" is a differential for pelvic pain. It is associated with:', '["Endometriosis", "PID (Chlamydia/Gonorrhea) causing perihepatitis", "Fibroids", "None"]', 1, 'Causes "violin-string" adhesions to the liver. (Beckmann)'),
    ('111: On histology, what is required for the definitive diagnosis of endometriosis?', '["Only glands", "Endometrial glands AND stroma (and often hemosiderin-laden macrophages)", "Cancer cells", "None"]', 1, 'Must see both components of the endometrium. (Williams)'),
    ('112: "Adenomyosis" and "Endometriosis" coexist in what percentage of cases?', '["0%", "10-20%", "~40-60%", "100%"]', 2, 'Commonly found together in women with chronic pelvic pain. (Comprehensive Gynecology)'),
    ('113: Which of the following is an "estrogen-independent" symptom sometimes seen in endometriosis?', '["There are none", "Neuropathic pain due to nerve infiltration", "Cyclic bleeding", "None"]', 1, 'Chronic pain can become centralized over time. (Williams)'),
    ('114: A 48-year-old with severe endometriosis is undergoing Hysterectomy + BSO. Should she receive Estrogen Replacement Therapy (HRT) immediately?', '["No, wait 6 months", "Yes, but combined Estrogen-Progestin is preferred to prevent \"unopposed\" stimulation of residual implants", "Never", "None"]', 1, 'Prevents recurrence in residual disease. (ACOG; Williams)'),
    ('115: "Endometriotic" implants on the diaphragm are more common on which side?', '["Left", "Right", "Equally distributed", "None"]', 1, 'Due to the clockwise flow of peritoneal fluid. (Williams; Comprehensive Gynecology)'),
    ('116: The "Powder-burn" appearance of endometriosis is due to:', '["Fresh blood", "Hemosiderin deposits from old hemorrhage trapped under the peritoneum", "Infection", "None"]', 1, 'Represents chronic, scarred disease. (Beckmann; Blueprints)'),
    ('117: Which imaging finding is MOST suggestive of bowel endometriosis?', '["Gas", "Mushroom-cap sign or \"pull-sign\" on MRI/Barium enema", "Normal scan", "None"]', 1, 'Indicates deep infiltration into the muscularis. (Williams)'),
    ('118: Is "Endometrial Ablation" a treatment for endometriosis?', '["Yes", "No, it treats heavy bleeding but has no effect on ectopic implants outside the uterus", "It is the best cure", "None"]', 1, 'Should not be confused with laparoscopic ablation of endometriosis. (Beckmann)'),
    ('119: A 24-year-old with endometriosis and dyschezia. Which exam step is crucial?', '["Speculum only", "Rectovaginal exam", "Breast exam", "None"]', 1, 'To palpate nodules in the rectovaginal septum. (ACOG; Beckmann)'),
    ('120: "Primary Dysmenorrhea" (without endometriosis) is caused by:', '["Psychological issues", "Excess Prostaglandin F2-alpha production during menses causing uterine contractions", "Cancer", "None"]', 1, 'Responds well to NSAIDs. (Blueprints; Beckmann)'),
    ('121: How does the pain of "Secondary" dysmenorrhea (Endometriosis) usually differ from "Primary"?', '["Primary is constant", "Secondary often begins days BEFORE menses and lasts longer", "Primary is more severe", "None"]', 1, 'Primary usually starts just with the onset of flow. (Beckmann; Williams)');
    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation)
